// Conquord subject: src/fiat2/eval/Jenkins_ItemDeletion.v
//
// The whole-row membership test.  There is no key -- the question is about a
// row -- so no dictionary can be keyed for it, and TableRep (the table
// represented AS a set) is the only index in Conquord that applies.  Cozy
// should pick a hash set, which is the same answer; this spec is here as a
// parity check on the one read whose index is a representation choice rather
// than a key choice.
//
// Encoding decision, found by running it: Cozy's `Set` carries a uniqueness
// invariant and rejected `register` as possibly breaking it, so the
// precondition is declared.  Jenkins's `HashSet.add` is idempotent and needs
// no precondition, and `ItemDeletion.register` is in fact only called for
// items not yet in flight, so the assume is faithful -- but it is an
// obligation the spec writer has to discover and discharge, which is the
// kind of thing differentiator 1 is about.
//
// Encoding decision: rows carry two fields because the read is whole-row
// equality.  With one field the test would be a membership test on a key,
// which a dictionary could serve, and the subject exists to isolate the read
// that no dictionary can serve.
ItemDeletion:

    type Item = { name : String, parent : String }

    state registrations : Set<Item>

    query contains(i : Item)
        i in registrations

    op register(i : Item)
        assume not (i in registrations);
        registrations.add(i);

    op deregister(i : Item)
        registrations.remove(i);
